{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CenpaAVU7AZWHLiYGtSnG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I reviewed the two repairs against the supplied source only. No commands were run; every claim below is from reading the diff and the quoted source, and I flag explicitly where a claim needs a check I could not perform.\n\n## Assessment\n\nBoth repairs are structurally sound and each fixes a real hole:\n\n- **Coordinator refusal.** The prior state was genuinely unsafe: a one-time `OSError` at import permanently disabled drift detection for the life of the process, and `running_scripts_moved()`'s `except OSError: return True` meant a *persistently* unreadable tree produced EXIT_RESTART on every poll → relaunch → capture fails → detection off forever. With the guard, that chain terminates after exactly one relaunch at a typed preflight exit that launchers don't re-arm. Generic importers still degrade rather than crash. That's the right shape.\n- **Need-ref narrowing.** The removed `registry_ids` docstring itself scopes the disagreement it existed to settle to *\"what a `#fragment` on the canonical need carrier may name.\"* Narrowing to the need carrier therefore discharges the stated obligation rather than dodging it — I do not challenge the scope narrowing. It also *fixes* a latent bug: `frozenset(found) or None` made an **empty** need registry read as \"unknown, don't judge,\" so every SN fragment passed; `need_ids(load_needs(...))` now judges it. The new empty-registry test locks that.\n\nWhat blocks approval is smaller than either repair: the new `names_carrier` helper adds a normalization the repair does not need, and that addition introduces a *fresh* H1/R-E divergence — the exact failure class this work exists to close.\n\n## Findings\n\n**1. (Blocking) `names_carrier`'s backslash folding creates a new H1/R-E divergence on POSIX.** `spine_carrier.py:482` does `str(rel_path).replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\")` unconditionally. On POSIX a backslash is a legal filename byte, so `plan_briefs._canonical_need_ref(\"docs\\\\requirements\\\\stakeholder-needs.md#SN-1\")` now returns `SN-1` (the new `tests/test_hats.py` case asserts exactly this). But `check_trajectory.specref_findings` computes `target = root / pathpart` and returns `\"does not resolve to an in-repo file\"` at `check_trajectory.py:2861` before ever reaching `names_carrier` — on POSIX that path does not exist. Same string, same box: H1 accepts, R-E rejects. Before this diff both rejected it. The helper's own docstring says *\"Registry references use POSIX paths on disk\"* — which makes the folding unnecessary by contract. **Fix by removal, not by patching R-E:** drop `.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\")` from both lines and delete the backslash assertion; keep only the `PurePosixPath` `./`-collapsing, which is the behavior the repair actually needs.\n\n**2. (Blocking) The changed hats test drops coverage of the real regression it cites.** `tests/test_hats.py` still carries the comment about the composer refusing the root-relative spelling *\"before 2026-09-06 while R-E resolved it clean\"*, but the assertion moved from `./docs/...` to `././docs/...`. The single-`./` spelling is the documented failure; `././` is not a form anyone writes. Assert both — the doubled form is a fine bonus, not a replacement.\n\n**3. `specref_findings` can now abort the entire check run on a malformed needs registry.** `load_needs` raises `SystemExit` when the TOML does not parse (`spine_carrier.py:958`) and `resolve` refuses a dual `.md`/`.toml` carrier. The deleted `registry_ids` docstring stated the contract being replaced: *\"None is 'unknown, do not judge' (the same contract as a markdown target's unreadable anchor set).\"* A findings function that raises kills every *other* finding in the run, so an unparseable needs file now masks all unrelated R-E output. This is likely already unreachable because check_trajectory almost certainly loads needs earlier — **please confirm that**, and if it is not already fatal upstream, either guard the call or let it emit a finding. Either way, the brief's malformed/empty/readable trichotomy is only tested for *empty*; add the malformed case.\n\n**4. Confirm the refusal covers every coordinator entry, not just `agent_loop.main`.** `agent_common.py:138` says the capture happens *\"before either coordinator loop starts\"* — \"either\" names two. The guard sits in one `main()`. Drive mode is reached through `_drive_entry` at `agent_loop.py:4522`, i.e. after the guard, so it is covered; the second loop the comment refers to needs to be identified and confirmed to route through `agent_loop.main` as well. If it does not, the new docstring claim *\"agent_loop refuses that unknown identity before it enters a coordinator role\"* is false for that path.\n\n**5. The unavailable-capture sentinel now has two homes.** `agent_loop.py:4502` reads `agent_common.LAUNCHED_SCRIPTS_FINGERPRINT is None` and re-derives what `None` means. `agent_common` already owns that contract in `running_scripts_moved`. A one-line `agent_common.drift_detection_available()` keeps the sentinel's meaning in the module that defines it and makes finding #4's audit a grep for one name. This is the kit's own \"state it in one home\" rule applied to the repair.\n\n**6. Verify no surviving references to `kitlib.spine.registry_ids`.** It is removed from both `__all__` and the module body. The supplied diff shows one caller; any other importer (a test, the dual-plan composer, `docs/registry-machinery-reference.md`) breaks at import or goes stale. Also confirm the pre-existing `test_specref_table_path_fragment_on_a_registry_is_a_finding` targets the *needs* carrier — if it uses any other registry, it now exercises the anchor path and its \"names no such row\" assertion cannot hold.\n\n**7. The narrowing test asserts less than the change does.** `test_specref_does_not_treat_nested_config_tables_as_registry_rows` covers `pyproject.toml`, but the behavior change is broader: a fragment on *any* non-need TOML — including `interfaces.toml#IF-0068` — is now unjudged and returns clean. That is the accepted trade, but it should be pinned by an assertion on a real spine registry with a comment saying it is deliberate, or the next reader will assume ID validation still applies to the other registries.\n\n## Notes (not findings)\n\n- Ordering in `main()` is correct: `_utf8_console()` precedes the guard, so the em dash in the refusal message is safe on a Windows console.\n- The refusal message does not name the unreadable path, but the import-time `agent_common` warning carries the `OSError` (which normally includes the filename) to the same stream. Adequate.\n- The trade-off of turning a transient scan race into a hard human-gated stop is the right call given `push = \"human\"`, and it is a stop rather than a loop. Not contested.\n- The new-test structure of driving `al.main()` with `parse_args`/`_resolve_root` stubbed does exercise the guard, but it would pass regardless of guard *placement*; it does not demonstrate the refusal precedes state mutation. Low priority given the guard sits third in the function.\n\nPer your instruction, I have not treated the passing test set as evidence, and I did not review the `check_trajectory` renderer-package hunk."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24884,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":24884},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5256cdc0-3ff1-4f28-bbbe-9dca0e9a29f7","uuid":"780e1dee-25f2-4993-92c0-d1e6634006f2","request_id":"req_011Cenpa9WgSz2FR1ed3FBef"}
